Deputy Prime Minister Neth Savoeun and Deputy Prime Minister Sar Sokha Disseminate Action Plan on Anti-Illegal Drugs Assignment
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Neth Savoeun, Deputy Prime Minister and Chairman of the National Authority for Combating Drugs and Dr. Sar Sokha, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Interior, presided over the dissemination of an action plan on the implementation of the implementation plan on May 3, 2024. Work to fight illegal drugs. The promotion ceremony was held at the Ministry of Interior.
On April 1, Deputy Prime Minister Neth Savoeun issued a directive on measures to implement the Royal Government Regulation No. 01 BOP dated 2 February 2024 on drastic measures to combat illegal drugs in Ministries, Institutions and Sub-National Administration.
Deputy Prime Minister Neth Savoeun issued four guidelines, including: * First: the use of test equipment * Second: How to test * Third: About the confirmation of test results * Fourth: For those who are positive about drugs.
The launch of the ninth campaign after the Royal Government of Cambodia saw that the implementation of the plan to fight against illegal drugs in the past, law enforcement forces, institutions and relevant authorities have been actively working to fight crime. Drugs are as hot as rain, with remarkable results.
Based on the results of the implementation of the past eight anti-drug campaign plans (2017-2023), law enforcement forces launched a total of 55,305 drug crime operations and arrested 120,307 suspects. Seized all kinds of drugs weighing about 27,641 kilograms and 520,005 kilograms of chemical compounds, as well as other means, materials and exhibits.
The police investigated, found and cracked down on drug crimes in 435 cases, arrested 164 ringleaders and 811 network members, and built a case to be sent to court to be punished according to the law.
At the same time, Cambodia also received support and cooperation from all national and international circles, especially large cases and cases of drug processing were suppressed and reduced to almost no activity in 2023. In addition, the continued search and crackdown on money laundering related to drug offenses, by freezing the assets of drug offenders in large numbers, has also contributed to Cambodia leaving the FATF's gray list.
