(Phnom Penh): After living as the leader of a Cambodian ship for nearly 40 years with great success, moving the country from the ashes of war to a prosperous country under the progress of development on the evening of May 1, 2024, Samdech Techo Hun Sen tasted leadership of a group of fighters on the battlefield for a humanitarian cause.
During the competition, as the honorary coach, Samdech Techo Hun Sen often discussed with Mr. Chhin Chhoeun, the coach. Some football fans really want to know what strategies Samdech Techo used to win the match yesterday?
The advantage of a 3-4-3 formation is that it focuses on service and combat. Both wingers play an important role in both the defense and the midfield.
Mr. Chhin Chhoeun said that the strategy that Samdech used in the above form is more than 3 to 4 strategies. Initially, Samdech Techo used the strategy for the first 5 minutes to 7 minutes, when the players are full and the opponents do not know the strategy is to hit the ball quickly to score. But this strategy did not succeed because the opponent's speed was so fast that the strategy was suspended.
Samdech Techo then offered a second strategy related to combat. Seeing that his opponent's right wing was so strong, Cam could not score. Therefore, Samdech Techo advised to open the fight from the opponent's left wing. Finally, the attack from the left wing also scored a goal.
Samdech Techo also gave the strategy to the above striker, Cam and Seng Mongkul to cooperate in the fight, but the ball was thrown a lot to Seng Mongkul kicked to the goal because Cam fell. To support this fighting movement, Samdech Techo also advised to push a fast person to help Cam to get the ball to Seng Mongkul. Finally, this strategy also helped Seng Mongkul to score the second goal.
With 20 minutes left in the match, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also instructed the players to drop the fight by coming down to defend as much as possible. In this strategy, Samdech Techo saw that in 20 minutes, the opponent could not score up to 2 goals, if possible, he could only score 1 goal, so the team did not lose. Even winning just one goal is good because it is a friendly match.
As a former national team player, Mr. Chhin Chhoeun expressed his admiration for Samdech Techo who has a lot of understanding on football, even though Samdech has never been a professional player.
The Football for Humanity event, led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, also became the record-breaking event for the most fundraising sports event in Cambodia ever.
The charity football event raised more than $ 5 million. Samdech Techo donated $ 500,000 to Angkor Hospital for Children, $ 300,000 to the Japanese Heart Pediatric Center, $ 200,000 to the families of the victims of the Kampong Speu bombing and more than $ 4 million to Kantha Bopha Hospital. .
Samdech Techo Hun Sen emphasized that this competition shows the achievements of peace, political stability and development, especially showing the spirit of unity among the Cambodian people.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen thanked the four football teams for participating in the competition. Samdech Techo also thanked all the artists who participated in organizing the work, especially Phnom Penh Capital Hall and all kinds of armed forces.
Samdech Techo also thanked the fans who attended both at the stadium and watched the live broadcast. Thank you to all the philanthropists who contributed money.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen emphasized that the final result of this competition is the life, health and smile of the children.
The ADB press release said developing regions in the Asia-Pacific region were not yet ready to ensure the well-being of the rapidly aging elderly population, which was apparently facing growing challenges. These include low pension insurance, health problems, social isolation, and limited access to essential services.
While this longer life reflects the success of regional development, it calls for urgent comprehensive policy reforms to support the well-being of the elderly. This is according to Asia's longevity and well-being: The Asian Development Policy Report released today by the Asian Development Bank (ADB) at its 57th Annual Meeting.
The number of people aged 60 and over in developing countries in Asia and the Pacific is projected to almost double by 2050 to 1.2 billion, or about a quarter of the total population. Growing demand for pension and welfare programs as well as health care services.
Physical and mental health problems also increase with age. About 60% of the elderly Asia-Pacific population does not attend or receive regular medical check-ups, while 31% have symptoms of depression due to social isolation and economic insecurity. Older women are more vulnerable than older men to poor health, including depression, diabetes and high blood pressure.
By making mandatory retirement age more flexible, helping older people stay healthy and providing them with decent employment opportunities, as well as lifelong learning and skills development, regional countries can help people Older people maintain productivity for a long time.
