(Battambang): Mr. Sok Lou, Provincial Governor and Chairman of the Battambang Provincial Committee of the Cambodian Red Cross, on the afternoon of May 2, 2024, chaired a meeting on "Together with the Cambodian Red Cross for a healthy and healthy community. Resistant to the weather "to celebrate the 161st anniversary of the World Red Cross and Red Crescent Day, May 8, 2024.
The outreach meeting was also attended by the Deputy Governors of the three Armed Forces, the heads of departments around the provinces, administrations, districts and a total of nearly 100 branch officers at the Battambang Provincial Hall.
At the beginning, Mr. Sok Lou brought a message from Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian Red Cross to the whole ceremony and expressed his deep gratitude for the responsible and responsible efforts. Participate in the support of all philanthropists and teams to achieve the expected success.
Mr. Sok Lou also presented the purpose of the meeting, such as disseminating to the public what the Cambodian Red Cross has been doing. Demonstrate the achievements and progress of the Cambodian Red Cross branch in Battambang province. To raise funds to continue humanitarian operations, assist vulnerable people and participate in addressing challenges.
He added that according to the Ministry of Health, the four non-communicable diseases include cancer, diabetes, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and cardiovascular disease, which account for almost half of all deaths. All in Cambodia.
Mr. Sok Lou added that in order to successfully raise funds for May 8, 2024, Battambang Branch hopes that philanthropists will contribute material support for the branch to achieve the vision and mission of the Cambodian Red Cross. Mobilize humanitarian forces to rescue the most vulnerable and vulnerable people through the four core sectors and adhere to the seven fundamental principles of the Red Cross and Red Crescent Movement.
(Phnom Penh): In celebration of National Midwifery Day, May 5, 2024 under the theme "Strengthen the midwifery profession to improve health care services for mothers, infants and young people in the context of climate change" on May 2, 2024 Professor Chheang Ra, Minister of Health, made six recommendations to health officials and relevant partners to improve the provision of services to the people more effectively.
Six recommendations include:
* All public and private educational institutions that are training students in the field of health, please update the curriculum to meet the standards and incorporate new key interventions, especially to strengthen the capacity of laboratories, demonstration centers, equip modern learning materials.
* Institutions must seek to mobilize resources to train teachers to increase their capacity by having high qualifications, virtues and good ethics.
* Health facilities that accept interns must focus on training students' practical skills.
* Continuing training during the work of midwives, governors, monitoring performance is very important and must be continued on a regular basis.
* All midwives must be registered with the Professional Council, must meet all conditions for obtaining a license, as well as renew a valid work license and must adhere to professional ethics.
* In hot weather, please call to take care of personal health and family, especially care for pregnant women, infants, children, the elderly as provided. On health precautions during hot weather "on April 7, 2024.
In celebration of International Midwifery Day, May 5, 2024, a ceremony was held to award medals to the leaders of health officials who have worked hard to provide health services to the people.
The above ceremony was also attended by Mrs. Pum Chantyny, Secretary General of the Cambodian Red Cross and High Representative of Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian Red Cross, together with the Secretary of State, Director of the Department of Health and the representative of the World Health Organization. Development partner, civil servants and members of the Midwifery Committee.
The Minister also requested the Deputy Governor of the Board of Governors in charge of Health, Director of the Health Department of the Capital-Provincial Administration to examine the possibility of applying for medals to civil servants and health workers from the health center level to the department. Health, based on the principle of proper application in accordance with the set procedures.
It should also be noted that the awarding of medals and medals will be organized to encourage health officials at all levels who have done their job.
