Vietnamese National Assembly to hold extraordinary session on Thursday
In April, the Vietnamese National Assembly convened an extraordinary session to accept President Vo Van Thuong's unexpected resignation. According to the Communist Party of Vietnam, the two Vietnamese leaders resigned for similar reasons: misconduct and incompetence, and this comes at a time when the ASEAN country is continuing its anti-corruption campaign.
Aside from discussing Hue's immediate resignation, it is unclear whether a decision or appointment will be made during Thursday's extraordinary session, as Vietnam still needs to find a solution. And approving the person to be the permanent replacement for both the President and the President of the National Assembly.
There is no supreme leader in Vietnam, officially led by four pillars: the General Secretary of the Communist Party, the President, the Prime Minister and the Speaker of the National Assembly. After all, the General Secretary of the Communist Party is the most powerful position in Vietnam.
In the past, Hue, 67, has been seen as a possible successor to the post of General Secretary of the Communist Party of Vietnam in the future. The National Assembly will hold its regular session for several weeks from May 20.
