(Kampong Cham): Mr. Un Chanda, Governor of Kampong Cham Province and Chairman of the Provincial Disaster Management Committee, on the morning of April 30, 2024, chaired a meeting on the preparation for intervention and response to the shortage of clean water.
After hearing the report from the relevant institutional units, Mr. Un Chanda, Governor of Kampong Cham Province, put the following measures:
* First, clean water operators must make every effort to be responsible for finding groundwater and groundwater sources that are close to supply to the people living in their service areas to adequately supply (alternate), if any. Deficiency.
* Second, water operators must prepare rubber tents for water storage in their service areas in collaboration with local authorities at all levels, relevant departments and NGOs to find water trucks to find water sources. Nearby for transporting water to distribute to people.
* Third: Water operators must educate people living in their service area to increase attention by preparing jars, tanks and water storage tanks for daily use and saving. Highest savings.
* Fourth: The Kampong Cham Water Supply Authority must prepare water tankers and water for delivery to people living in its service area and help other areas as needed.
* Sixth: Municipal-district administrations must cooperate with the Department of Industry, Science, Technology and Innovation to inspect the preparation sites to prepare water storage facilities in villages that face water shortages to transport water to storage for distribution to people in the geographical area. Lack of clean water to use.
* Seventh: Municipal-district administrations, departments and units around the province continue to follow the instructions of the provincial administration on fire prevention measures and preparedness interventions in response to the shortage of clean water for daily use of people and animals in the local area. Risk of water shortage.
* Eighth: The municipal-district administration must advise the people about the cultivation of various mixed crops that use large amounts of water from natural water sources (except rivers) must be reduced and suspended to save natural water sources for humans and animals. Vehicles for daily use.
The concrete bridge across the spillway in Anlong Veng district, donated by Samdech Maniphea Hun Manet through the Ministry of Water Resources, will be opened in July.
(Oddar Meanchey): A concrete bridge under construction on a diversion dam in Trapeang Tao commune, Anlong Veng district, Oddar Meanchey province is scheduled to be officially opened for people to cross and transport agricultural products in July 2024. .
Mr. Nhen Kuon, Director of Oddar Meanchey Provincial Department of Water Resources and Meteorology, said on the occasion that he led the team to inspect the construction of the bridge this morning, April 30, 2024, that the concrete bridge under construction is a gift from Samdech Moha Bovathibodi Hun Manet, Prime Minister of Cambodia, through Mr. Tho Chetha, Minister of Water Resources and Meteorology, as a bond for the people of Oddar Meanchey province.
The director of the department said that the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology has been busy building irrigation infrastructure throughout the country in order to increase the irrigation capacity of rice and other mixed crops, as well as increase water resources for all uses. Sector.
For Oddar Meanchey province, the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology is constructing a concrete bridge 125 meters long, 7 meters wide and 5.5 meters high on the construction of a new diversion dam in Trapeang Tao commune, Anlong Veng district, which is under construction by the Department of Engineering, Ministry of Water Resources. It is now 55% complete.
According to the director of the department, the concrete bridge under construction is very important to connect from one area to another, especially in Anlong Veng district, Chong Kal district, Samrong city, Oddar Meanchey province and Srey Snom district, Siem Reap province. Such as transporting agricultural products to the market as well.
He added that before the concrete bridge was built on the spillway, during the rainy season, it was very difficult for people in the area to travel, as well as to transport agricultural products to the market, students traveling to school had to cross the river. Boat rides, tractor rides, and sometimes the floodwaters on the spillway have caused people to drown and sometimes endanger people.
