Ministry of Tourism urges Hoka project to expand training to 25 capitals-provinces and key priority skills in line with current and future labor market needs
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Tourism, Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, the Cambodian Tourism Association, relevant ministries and partner organizations have expressed satisfaction with the results and effectiveness of the Hokka Skills Training and urged the project to expand the Hoka Skills Training to 25. Capital-province and by key priority skills in line with current and future labor market needs.
It should be informed that the Ministry of Tourism has organized "Hoka Skills Day 2024" under the theme: Investment, tourism, investment in human resources at Sokha Hotel Phnom Penh throughout April 29, 2024 with about 300 participants.
At the Closing Ceremony of Hoka Skills Day 2024, Mr. Pak Sokhom, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Tourism, said that Hoka Skills Day 2024 is a forum to share experiences and good ideas on technical and vocational training in line with the strategic direction of the Royal Government of the seventh mandate. Aims to promote the development of high quality and productive human resources, especially the strategy of training 1.5 million young people.
Mr. Pak Sokhom stated, "As a result, the Hoka Project Management Committee of the Ministry of Tourism has trained a total of 4,556 tourism professionals in the fields of: housekeeping, front office, food and beverage service, coffee, chefs, community tour guides, trainers and evaluators." In the second phase of 2020-2024.
On behalf of Mr. Sok Soken, Minister of Tourism, Mr. Tith Chantha, Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Tourism, considered "Hoka Skills Day 2024" as a potential mechanism to enhance the ownership of training of sub-national administrations and promote Encourage the tourism industry association and the private sector and stakeholders to actively participate in the implementation of the "Development 3" initiative.
The Permanent Secretary of State recommended some tips for the successful and effective implementation of the Hokka Hospitality Training Program:
1. Continue to work closely with Swiss Kanthak, development partners, tourism industry associations, the private sector and stakeholders to expand Hokka's training to 25 capitals and provinces across the country and in key priority areas in line with current and future labor market needs. .
3. The Capital-Provincial Administration would like to support and promote the training of Hoka Hospitality at the sub-national level in order to strengthen the quality of the province's tourism services and provide decent employment opportunities and income growth to the youth.
5. The Department of Tourism of all project target provinces must continue to work hard to structure, implement the project effectively, efficiently and transparently, and promote the capacity of trainers of the Department of Tourism and the private sector to ensure the sustainability of post-project training.
6. The Hoka Project Management Committee needs to develop and update Hoka Hospitality training programs and training materials, especially to encourage the participation of women with green skills and to promote entrepreneurship in the tourism sector.
The ICC is investigating both Hamas cross-border attacks and a series of Israeli military responses in Gaza that have lasted for seven months.
In response to Israeli media reports that the ICC may issue arrest warrants for senior Israeli government and military officials soon, Israeli Foreign Minister Katz on Sunday warned the Israeli embassy to increase security. Of them, warning of the risk of "a wave of serious anti-religious".
Israeli media reported that Israeli officials were concerned that a court might issue arrest warrants for Netanyahu and other senior officials over alleged violations of international humanitarian law in the Gaza Strip.
The ICC is also considering issuing an arrest warrant for Hamas leaders, the source said.
Israel is not a member of the tribunal and does not recognize its jurisdiction, but Palestine was accepted as a member state in 2015. In October, ICC prosecutor Karim Khan said the court had jurisdiction over possible war crimes committed by Hamas fighters in Israel and Israeli forces in the Gaza Strip.
