(Phnom Penh): The Information and Quick Reaction Team of the Phnom Penh Municipal Police on April 28, 2024 came out to clarify the remarks made by Ms. Try Dana regarding the law enforcement of the Sen Sok District Police Inspectorate.
Pursuant to the order of Lt. Gen. Chuon Narin, Deputy Commissioner and Phnom Penh Municipal Police Commissioner, at 11 pm on April 27, 2024, the police conducted an alcohol test on Hanoi Road in front of the new Phnom Penh Primary School. In Sangkat Phnom Penh Thmey, Khan Sen Sok.
At around 00:53 on April 28, 2024, the police found a white car with license plate Phnom Penh 2BS-2222 with two motorcycles, traveling from south to north. The police arrived at the checkpoint to check for alcohol, but the police signaled the driver of the car to enter the checkpoint, but the car did not cooperate to enter the checkpoint and turned left to enter the checkpoint. Check.
Suddenly, the police suspected that the car was driving away, so they chased after the car and stopped it and invited the driver to cooperate in the alcohol test, then the car owner agreed to cooperate and came to the checkpoint and the driver removed the mask. Only then did the police know that she was the husband of Mrs. Try Dana and he also cooperated with the police smoothly without any protest.
He then blew alcohol, resulting in no alcohol in his body. Authorities thanked the car owner for his cooperation and for allowing him to continue his journey.
In particular, since the authorities of Sen Sok district have implemented strict alcohol control until now, our authorities have never called to inspect Ms. Try Dana's car, only this time last night, at the above target, her husband He is the driver.
The enforcement of alcohol control is the enforcement force in accordance with the high order of the head of the Royal Government of Cambodia is aimed at people to respect the road traffic law to reduce traffic accidents and protect the lives and property of the people.
On the other hand, the inspection of alcohol at night, according to the report of the Phnom Penh Municipal Police, shows that the majority of people have obeyed the traffic law, especially observed that traffic offenses have decreased. Philippines requires students to study online temporarily due to heat wave
MANILA: The Philippines will require all public school students to study online for two days due to extreme heat waves and jeepney bus strikes across the country. This is according to the Philippine Ministry of Education, quoted by the AFP news agency.
In a message posted on Facebook on Sunday, April 28, the Philippine Ministry of Education said: All state halls across the country will be open for online training from April 29 to 30, 2024.
Unusual heat waves have battered the Philippines for weeks, forcing thousands of schools to open online, while the Philippine Ministry of Education is monitoring more than 47,000 schools across the country.
