Many Asian countries have been hit hard by the financial turmoil, which has attracted widespread attention from the international community.
It is not difficult to see the problem of Japan, the harvest of the US dollar against the yen now reflects the use of the international status of the US dollar by the United States. By harvesting other countries, it benefits its economy and achieves its economic interests and political goals.
The US dollar rate hike extends beyond Japan. The whole of Asia has become a victim of the US dollar rate hike. Tightening US monetary policy has prompted investors to pull out of the Asian economy.
Due to the depreciation of the yen, Thailand became the first country to be affected. There are about 5,400 Japanese companies operating in Thailand, and these Japanese companies account for 74% of Thailand's exports. Thailand's export industry is heavily dependent on Japanese companies.
If the current Japanese yen appreciation leads to the removal of large amounts of foreign capital, the Thai economy will be under severe pressure. This situation could pose a similar risk to the 1997 Asian financial crisis.
If Asian countries' secondary interest rates remain higher for a long time (because they do not want to cut interest rates before the central bank), the domestic economy will collapse. Pressure may rise, which is unfavorable to the stability of the Asian exchange rate.
The irresponsible rate hike and recession policy adopted by the US is likely to push the global financial markets into a cash crisis and increase the risk of stagnation and recession. Many factors, such as the sharp appreciation of the US dollar, the European energy crisis and rising global commodity prices, are linked to the growing pressure on the global economy.
Mr. Peng Po Sa emphasized that these achievements are the result of the win-win policy of Samdech Techo Hun Sen and are currently continuing to maintain, protect and develop under the wise and skillful leadership of Samdech Prime Minister Hun Manet, Prime Minister of Cambodia. Cambodia.
