General knowledge: The original rule in the ceremony of washing the feet of the bride or relatives to wash the feet of the groom
(Phnom Penh): The procedure of washing the feet is very important as it is part of the wedding ceremony. It has been observed that the washing of the feet was claimed by European-influenced Cambodians as degrading to women by this opinion, and the foot-washing ceremony was abolished in most weddings. Cambodians who practice Christianity have the bride wash the groom's feet, while the groom has to wash the bride's feet away from the original Khmer tradition.
Cambodians who do not know and those who are influenced by Europe understand that the rights of men and women are equal. Therefore, if the bride has to wash her feet, the bridegroom must also wash his feet. They are too delicate with European influence, do not understand Khmer philosophy.
Wanting to give up foot washing is a misconception. Khmer has given value to women, and by understanding the value of women, Khmer has created some things, such as for men to get engaged to women, for men to serve women when married, for women to hold money, and so on.
It is seen that the wedding ceremony should be based on the record of Preah Chey Chesta and rewritten by Duke Moha Pheakdey Nhek Nou and Udom Prey Chea Chapin. Easy to see. It is advisable to do this foot washing ceremony in accordance with the power of the bride or groom to wash the groom's feet.
Perhaps because of the ingenuity of the King, who gave the foot-washers a younger brother or sister instead of a daughter, the washing of the feet was able to maintain both tradition and avoid the misconception that Lower the value of women.
