Article 41 of the Cambodian Constitution states that Cambodian citizens have freedom of expression, freedom of the press, freedom of the press, and freedom of assembly. No one shall be subjected to arbitrary interference with his privacy, family, home or correspondence, nor to attacks upon his honor and reputation. The press regime is regulated by law.
Article 22 (2) of the International Covenant stipulates that the exercise of this right may be restricted only by law and necessary in a democratic society in the interests of national security or public safety, public order, protection of public health or morals or Protect the rights and freedoms of others. This article does not preclude legal restrictions on military or police members exercising this right.
Article 29 (2) of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights states that in exercising their rights and freedoms, all persons shall be limited to the limits set by law in order to ensure the recognition and respect of the rights and freedoms of others. And to properly meet the moral, public order and general well-being needs of a democratic society.
