TOKYO (Reuters) - Japanese banks have increased their resilience to interest rate hikes by rebalancing their portfolios, the Bank of Japan said in a report on the financial system on Thursday. Domestic debt, but increased risk to real estate can pose a risk. According to Reuters.
The latest analysis of risks in the financial system by the Bank of Japan comes after the central bank last month ended negative interest rates that have been in force for eight years.
The central bank said all banks had reduced their holdings of yen-denominated bonds, reducing interest-related risks, especially long-term bonds, Reuters reported, citing a mid-year report. Long time no see.
The central bank said that the cost of obtaining credit from these banks continued to decline despite the increase in the number of bankruptcies due to labor shortages, as small companies accounted for the largest share of bankruptcy in the country. Recently.
The report also noted that an improved macroeconomic outlook and rising interest rates could be expected to lead to an improvement in household income and interest rate balance.
"Overall, banks in Japan have sufficient capital and a stable fund base to cope with stress," the report said. Among the potential risks, the Bank of Japan cited the emerging risks of these banks in relation to real estate, especially in urban areas. According to Reuters, Kratie provincial governor sent a representative to bring money and materials to 11 people poisoned noodles.
