Human resources remain a challenge for Vietnamese tourism
According to Vietnam News, the Vietnam National Tourism Authority (VNAT) has 195 tourism training institutes, including 65 universities, 55 colleges, 71 vocational schools, four vocational training centers and institutions. 2 operated by an enterprise. All establishments can provide about 20,000 tourists a year, while the Vietnamese tourism sector needs 40,000.
Of the tourism workforce, only 43 percent have vocational training in tourism, and only 9.7 percent have a university degree or postgraduate degree.
A report released by the Vietnam Tourism Development Research Institute shows that the quality and productivity of labor in the Vietnamese tourism industry is still low. For example, hotel productivity in Vietnam is only one-15th compared to Singapore, one-tenth compared to Japan and one-fifth compared to Malaysia. As a result, Vietnamese tourism staff face stiff competition in their homeland.
Many tourists from countries such as the Philippines, Thailand, Indonesia and Singapore have come to work in Vietnam, and most 4- and 5-star hotels in Vietnam are hiring foreign staff.
With the strong recovery of tourism in the aftermath of the Kovid-19 pandemic, many tourism businesses have resumed and more modern accommodation facilities are operating, exacerbating the shortage of skilled tourism personnel. . According to Vietnam News, a gas tank exploded, setting fire to five houses in Preah Vihear province's Chey Sen district.
