Indonesia amends import law after complaints from business groups
JAKARTA: Indonesia is revising regulations set to regulate the import of more than 3,000 products amid concerns from business groups that the law could disrupt the chain, the Indonesian Minister of Commerce said on Wednesday. Domestic supply and export. According to Reuters.
Southeast Asia's largest economy issued regulations late last year aimed at tightening controls on imports of goods ranging from food ingredients to electronics and chemicals due to concerns over inflows. Goods.
Importers are required to obtain import licenses for various goods, including footwear, textiles and washing machines, and require technical guidance from other government agencies to obtain such permits.
In a separate statement, the Indonesian Ministry of Economic Coordination said the government was also suspending the requirements for technical documents to obtain permits. The ministry did not specify when the suspension would take place.
The Ministry of Commerce has said it will allow a transitional phase for new regulations when the revised regulations are issued.
