Indonesia launches measures to curb imports of electricity into its markets
JAKARTA: The Indonesian Ministry of Industry has issued restrictions on the import of electronic goods, including air conditioners, refrigerators and laptops, as a measure aimed at improving investment realities. According to Reuters.
The move follows regulations issued late last year by Southeast Asia's largest economy to tighten controls on imports ranging from food ingredients and electronics to chemicals.
Reuters reports that the regulations, dated Feb. 1 but announced Monday, were issued in response to protests by business associations over the impact on domestic markets due to the influx of imported goods, Reuters reported. .
According to the regulations, the aim is to develop the local electronics sector, and some products will require import permits and investigator reports.
Priyadi said that by 2023, Indonesia's electrical and electronics industry will produce 1.2 million air conditioners, with a capacity of 2.7 million. Indonesia imported 3.8 million air conditioners last year. According to Reuters.
