In our Khmer civilization, water was used for sprinkling water for blessing and cooling. In Khmer, we have a ceremony to worship God, and watering during the Khmer New Year also evolves from this tradition of worshiping God. Many rituals are performed at pagodas, using clean water, spraying perfume or perfume with lotus or jasmine, and sprinkling incense on the Buddha statues.
At the same time, it is customary to draw water during the procession in the village to the pagoda, where people have prepared jars or buckets of water in front of the house for the monks to come and bless the owner. Or neighbors in the village on the streets.
According to the beliefs in our Cambodian society, people can also go to the pagoda to invite monks to water the zodiac. Traditional Khmer healers also use the watering of relief for believers or seek healing or divination. In particular, in our Cambodian family, many parents have also organized a watering ceremony to bless their children as well.
