According to the Secretary-General of the National Committee for the Mekong River of Cambodia, the Nam Khan, a tributary of the Mekong River, flows into the Mekong River at Luang Prabang, about a mile from the Cambodian-Lao border. , 370 km above Pakse city about 1,220 km and above Vientiane capital about 500 km. Below Luang Prabang, about 120 km, is the Xayaburi Dam on the Mekong River.
It should be noted that a truck carrying chemicals overturned and overflowed into the Nam Khan River in Phu Sang Kham village in Luang Prabang province, Laos. Luang Prabang City Hall has banned its residents from bathing, swimming or collecting dead fish from the Nam Khan River and from the Mekong River in Luang Prabang for sale or consumption.
Municipal authorities have instructed Phu Sang Kham village authorities to regularly inform and warn villagers about this issue.
According to officials at the MRC Secretariat in Vientiane, Laos, the chemical spill was about 30 tons of sulfuric acid, but not all of it flowed into the water. In this case, the Lao Department of Environment has cooperated with the Luang Prabang office to implement monitoring measures to respond to the above situation. However, it does not affect the lower Mekong River.
