At least 330 students take part in the Royal University of Phnom Penh Lifetime Achievement Award and expand their understanding to millions of students
(Phnom Penh): At least 330 students participated in the Lifetime Achievement Award competition under the theme "Human capital development is the lifeblood of Cambodia to achieve its 2030 vision and year. 2050 ”organized by the Department of Lifelong Learning of the Royal University of Phnom Penh (RUPP).
This program has been successfully organized three times since 2022, the last third just ended on the evening of April 6, 2024, while the third time with 110 groups of students. There are at least 330 students in each group.
Head of Department Moeun Chantrea said that the third Lifetime Achievement Award is divided into two stages:
* Stage 2: The 5 strongest teams will be presented directly in front of the committee on their work, as well as additional opinions of the team, as well as answer the questions of the committee to put the final score, so the committee The scorers in the first and second stages combine to make the final decision on the 1st, 2nd and 3rd place winners (Top 3).
Mr. Moeun Chantrea continued that the first place winner received a computer for improving the efficiency of their further studies. The 2nd place winner and the 3rd place winner will receive some other incentive funds. Groups 4 and 5 also received some incentives, respectively. For students from remote provinces, the committee also provides some travel expenses.
He stated that the organization of this Lifelong Education Award is to contribute to the dissemination and knowledge of the policy on lifelong learning of the Royal Government of Cambodia and for young people to increase the use of the national language through Writing in Khmer. It should be informed that the policy on lifelong learning has been implemented since 2019.
According to the head of the Department of Lifelong Education, based on the writings of each group of students, it shows more understanding, especially knowing and showing the pentagonal strategy of the Royal Government of the 7th legislature, which proves that the youth of Cambodia is monitoring and understanding the vision of the Royal Government, so they will further contribute to the implementation of this policy.
Kreng Chanpisey, Chan Sophanrabot and Khon Soknem, the first place winners, said that this was the first time they had participated in such a public competition, but the team was determined to do their best. Ability, no matter the result, everyone is exhausted, but the result is really unexpected.
Secretary of State Nous Sled praised the organization of this Lifelong Education Award and supported it in the coming years as a contribution to the Royal Government in developing human capital to lead to more socio-economic development.
The Secretary of State stated that participating in promoting lifelong learning will contribute to the government's vision of becoming a high middle income country by 2030 and a high income country by 2050.
