Indonesia revises import rules after complaints from businesses
JAKARTA: Indonesia is evaluating regulations designed to curb the import of more than 3,000 products after concerns that the rules could disrupt the chain, a senior Commerce Ministry official said on Friday. Domestic supply and disrupt exports. According to Reuters.
Reuters reports that Southeast Asia's largest economy issued regulations late last year to tighten controls on imports ranging from food ingredients to appliances and chemicals. . Indonesian authorities say they are responding to complaints from business associations about the impact on the domestic market regarding the influx of imported goods.
But since then, businesses have said the new law, which goes into effect on March 10, restricts access to some of the raw materials they need.
Following complaints and warnings about raw material shortages, the Commerce Ministry last month eased restrictions on aircraft parts and raw materials for the plastics industry. But those businesses say the law needs to be relaxed further.
The Indonesian Chamber of Commerce and Industry said inappropriate restrictions on goods could disrupt operations in export-oriented industries, including automobiles, refining and electronics manufacturing, food and beverage. According to Reuters.
