Cambodian Youth Attending UN Human Rights Council Meeting Proud to Represent the Country on Important Forum
(Phnom Penh): Ms. On Monita, a potential young leader from Cambodia who has been appointed as the Youth Ambassador for Peace, who is attending the 55th session of the UN Human Rights Council, is proud. Strongly represent the country on such an important forum.
Monita represented Cambodia in an informal consultation on strengthening the Economic and Social Council and a review of the High-Level Political Forum on Sustainable Development during a UN Human Rights Council meeting. Nation in Geneva on March 28, 2024.
"As the first person to attend the meeting as a Youth Ambassador for Peace from Cambodia, it is paving the way for other young leaders to follow this path," she said. "My presence shows hope, courage and determination to create a better world for all."
The selection of Monita is a historic event for Cambodia as she became the first youth representative from a country to attend a UN Human Rights Council meeting. Her participation underscores the international community's recognition of the valuable views that the young voice brings to the global dialogue on peace, development and human rights.
The Human Rights Council meeting is an important forum for discussing the strengthening of international cooperation and the promotion of human rights. As a young ambassador, Monita joins international leaders and contributes to key dialogues aimed at promoting sustainable development goals and promoting economic and social policies.
"This participation is not only a rare honor for her, but also a national pride for Cambodia," she said. "It represents the country's growing influence and commitment to actively participate in the world, especially in promoting the interests and rights of the younger generation." General knowledge: The formation of the oceans on Earth
The formation of the oceans About 4,500 million years ago, the earth was different from what it is today: there were no oceans and seas. Later, volcanoes spewed ash and gas all over the planet, causing the earth to become much hotter than it is today. Volcanic gas, combined with various gases and water vapor, began to form the atmosphere around the planet.
About 4,000 years ago, when the atmosphere evolved, the earth cooled enough, causing water vapor to condense and rain. Rainfall began to fill the lowlands and the earth's crust, eventually turning into oceans. The oceans have changed a lot according to the history of the planet.
