This was stated by Dr. Huot Chanyuda, President of the National Center for Tuberculosis Control and Hansen at the opening ceremony of the 27th National TB Control Conference under the chairmanship of Dr. Ing Phirun, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health, which took place on April 4. 2024 at the Phnom Penh Hotel.
Dr. Huot Chanyuda continued, "Despite our success, our country still has a lot of TB cases and there are some challenges that need to be addressed in order for the national program to achieve sustainable development goals." "SDG) by the end of 2030, in which we aim to reduce the incidence rate by 80% and the mortality rate by 90% by 2030 from the 2015 figure, which is in line with the strategy to end global TB."
At the same time, he called on all parties to help and continue to support the national program to achieve the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) by the end of 2030 and beyond.
On that occasion, Dr. Ing Phirun said that on behalf of the Ministry of Health, I would like to congratulate and highly appreciate this proud achievement. "But for TB control during 2024-2025, we will have a lot of work to do in line with the next TB strategy and sustainable development goals."
To achieve the goal of the New Year 2024 as well as the following year, the Secretary of State made five recommendations, including:
1: Efforts must be made to research more cases of tuberculosis of all kinds in accordance with the new direction of the national and international programs, namely access to global TB services.
2: Care must be taken to strengthen the implementation of policies to diagnose and treat TB free of charge in order to encourage patients to receive quality TB services as soon as possible.
3: Further efforts should be made to prevent and treat tuberculosis, pediatric TB, and in particular TB research among people living close to TB patients and high-risk groups, such as those living with HIV and diabetes. Elderly people and in prisons.
4: Continue to strengthen cooperation between drug-resistant TB and TB-AIDS to further reduce the incidence of illness and death due to this problem.
5. Seek additional resources in close collaboration with stakeholders.
